---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ImageTool from '../components/ImageTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize Image to KB Online, Free | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Reduce a photo to the file size your form allows, in KB, right in your browser. Preview, compare sizes and download. The code here does not upload your image.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune image resizer', '/', description);
---
<Base pageType="home" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Resize an image to a size in KB" lead="Pick the limit your form asks for, add your photo, download. You see a preview and the real before and after sizes, with no account and no watermark." />

  <ImageTool client:load defaultMaxKb={50} />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      SizeTune needs JavaScript, because the resizing happens inside your browser and not on a server. Turn JavaScript on
      and reload this page to use the tool. The explanation below is still readable without it.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <section aria-label="Why people trust SizeTune" class="mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    {[
      ['Nothing is uploaded', 'Your image is resized by code running on your own device.', 'M5 11h14v9H5zM8 11V8a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3'],
      ['Test it yourself', 'Once this page has loaded, switch off your internet. It still works.', 'M2 8.8a15 15 0 0 1 4-2.5M22 8.8a15 15 0 0 0-8.5-3.7M5 12.5a10 10 0 0 1 3.5-2M19 12.5a10 10 0 0 0-3-2M8.5 16a5 5 0 0 1 7 0M3 3l18 18'],
      ['Free, no strings', 'No sign-up, no watermark, no limit on how many images.', 'M12 3v18M17 7.5c0-1.9-2.2-3-5-3s-5 1.1-5 3 2.2 2.8 5 3.5 5 1.6 5 3.5-2.2 3-5 3-5-1.1-5-3'],
    ].map(([t, d, icon]) => (
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="flex size-10 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-teal-100 text-teal-900">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d={icon} /></svg>
        </span>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-950">{t}</p>
          <p class="mt-0.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">{d}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section aria-label="Start from the job you have" class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Start from the job you have</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600">Each page opens the tool with the right settings already chosen.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {[
        ['/resize-image-to-20kb/', 'Resize to 20 KB', 'The smallest common limit. Tips to keep faces readable.'],
        ['/resize-signature/', 'Resize a signature', 'Crop to the ink, put it on white, then shrink.'],
        ['/guides/photo-size-for-online-forms/', 'Photo size guide', 'Pixels, KB and formats, explained from scratch.'],
      ].map(([href, t, d]) => (
        <a href={href} class="block rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-sm hover:border-teal-700 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700">
          <span class="block font-semibold text-slate-950">{t}</span>
          <span class="mt-1 block text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">{d}</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-12 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>What the tool does</h2>
    <p>
      Many upload forms refuse a file that is even slightly over a limit written in kilobytes. A modern phone photo is
      usually several megabytes, so it has to shrink a lot before a form will take it. SizeTune does that shrinking
      without any software to install. You can drop a file onto the page or use the file picker, then set the maximum
      size with one of the presets (10, 20, 30, 50, 100 or 200 KB) or type your own number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some forms also set a minimum, for example a file that must be between 20 and 50 KB. You can enter a range
      instead of a single limit. The tool shows the original and the new file size, the pixel width and height before
      and after, and a before and after view so you can judge the picture yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>How a picture gets under the limit</h2>
    <p>
      There are two levers, and the tool lets you choose which one comes first. With quality-first, the pixels are kept for as long as possible and the compression is made stronger until the file fits; the picture is shrunk only if quality alone cannot reach the limit and you did not fix the dimensions. With dimensions-first, the
      picture is made smaller in pixels and the quality is kept higher. Quality-first suits a form that fixes the exact
      pixel size. Dimensions-first suits a form that only cares about the file size, because a smaller picture with
      cleaner detail often looks better than a large, heavily compressed one.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you want a specific width or height, type it in. The aspect lock keeps the picture from being stretched. An
      opaque PNG with a size limit is preselected as JPEG, with a note, and you can switch back to PNG. Every other file keeps its own format (JPEG, PNG or WebP) unless you choose another. WebP output is
      offered only where your browser can produce it. The tool can also be stopped part-way with the cancel button, and nothing is saved until
      you press download.
    </p>

    <h2>When a target cannot be reached</h2>
    <p>
      File size follows the amount of visual detail and the number of pixels. A large, busy photograph has a lot of
      both, so a very small limit forces either a strong drop in quality or a big drop in dimensions. At the other end,
      a very small and plain image, such as a tiny scan of a signature, may already be smaller than a minimum that your
      form sets, and no honest setting makes it larger in a useful way.
    </p>
    <p>
      In those cases SizeTune tells you what happened and shows the closest result it could produce, instead of
      presenting a file that misses the limit as a success. Exact sizes are never guaranteed. A result is judged by the
      number you see next to the file, so read it before you upload.
    </p>

    <h2>Pages for specific jobs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/resize-image-to-20kb/">Resize an image to 20 KB</a> opens the tool with a 20 KB maximum and explains
        how to get there with the least visible damage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/resize-signature/">Resize a signature</a> adds cropping and positioning for scanned or photographed
        signatures, and explains why a small signature can fall short of a minimum.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/photo-size-for-online-forms/">The photo size guide</a> explains pixels, kilobytes and file
        formats from the beginning.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      SizeTune does not know what any particular form requires. Always follow the instructions written on your own
      form or portal, because they can change from one application round to the next.
    </p>

    <h2>Your images stay in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The picture you choose is read and re-encoded by code running on your own device. SizeTune's code does not upload
      it, and this page loads no advertising or analytics scripts. Opening the page still sends the normal request that
      every website receives, which the hosting company handles. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> explains
      this in detail, and the <a href="/about/">about page</a> says who runs the site and how to reach them.
    </p>

  </div>

  <section aria-label="Frequently asked questions" class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 border-b border-slate-300">
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Is 20 KB the same as 20,000 bytes or 20,480 bytes?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">Both conventions are in use. SizeTune aims below your maximum counted as 1,000 bytes per KB, and above your minimum counted as 1,024, so the file passes whether a portal counts a KB as 1,000 or 1,024 bytes (except for ranges too narrow for both, which are flagged). Results are displayed in 1,024-byte KB, so a 20 KB target shows as up to about 19.5 KB, often a little lower.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Will my picture look worse?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">Usually a little, and sometimes a lot, depending on how small the limit is compared with the original. JPEG compression discards detail, and the tool looks for a high quality that still fits. The preview lets you compare before you download.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Is my photo sent anywhere?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">SizeTune's code keeps the image on your device and does not upload it. Browser extensions or device backup features that you have installed are outside the site's control.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Which pictures can I open?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">Only JPEG, PNG and WebP files are accepted. Other types, such as HEIC photos from some phones or GIF images, need converting first. On an iPhone you can set Settings, Camera, Formats to Most Compatible, or export the photo as a JPEG.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Why did the tool say it could not reach my limit?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">The limit is too small for that image at the chosen settings, or the minimum is too large for it. Try the dimensions-first option, allow a smaller width and height, choose a plainer picture, or raise the limit if your form permits it.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Does it work on a phone?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">It runs in current mobile browsers. Very large photos use a lot of memory, so an older phone may be slow or may fail on a huge file. If that happens, try again with a smaller original.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group border-t border-slate-300">
        <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2 text-[1.0625rem] font-semibold text-slate-950 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-teal-700 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          Does it cost anything?
          <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-5 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <p class="pb-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-700">No. There is no account, no sign-up and no watermark added by SizeTune.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
